.TH WRIT\-REVOKE 1 2026-10-12 "writ 0.1.0" "writ manual"
.SH NAME
writ\-revoke \- revoke a statement or signature
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nh
.ad l
\fBwrit revoke\fR \fB\-i\fR\ \fIDIR\fR [\fB\-\-key\fR\ \fIPATH\fR]... [\fB\-\-note\fR\ \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-q\fR] \fITARGET\fR
.ad
.hy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Revoke a statement or data signature that the identity in DIR issued. The commands that issue statements print the start of the hash; \fBwrit show DIR/issued.writ\fR prints all of them.
.PP
A revocation is final. To undo it, issue the statement again. Verifiers learn about a revocation only when they see it, so add it to the bundles you give them.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fITARGET\fR
the start of its hash (at least 6 hex digits), or a file with the item
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-identity\fR \fIDIR\fR
the identity that signs the statement
.TP
\fB\-\-key\fR \fIPATH\fR
secret key file (default: DIR/current.key); give one \-\-key per key when several keys must sign; '\-' means standard input
.TP
\fB\-\-note\fR \fITEXT\fR
text to show with the statement; writ does not use it
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
print only the statement
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print help; \fB\-h\fR is short, \fB\-\-help\fR is long
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
success; for questions, the answer is yes
.TP
1
the answer is no: the check, verification, proof or name lookup was denied
.TP
2
an error: bad usage, unreadable input, or a failed operation
.SH EXAMPLES
Revoke a grant:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ revoke \-i me 3fa2c1d0 >> bundle.writ
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBwrit\fR(1), \fBwrit\-show\fR(1), \fBwrit\-export\fR(1)
